"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,333\nEpisode 2\n\n3\n00:00:07,413 --> 00:00:09,480\nSleep on a schedule\n\n4\n00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:15,265\nTwo students each sleep about seven hours a night.\n\n5\n00:00:15,724 --> 00:00:18,344\nOne goes to bed at a different time every night.\n\n6\n00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:20,740\nThe other keeps a steady schedule.\n\n7\n00:00:20,992 --> 00:00:21,804\nWho does better?\n\n8\n00:00:22,173 --> 00:00:24,327\nThe research says timing matters,\n\n9\n00:00:24,347 --> 00:00:25,507\nnot just hours.\n\n10\n00:00:25,677 --> 00:00:31,640\nAt Harvard, researchers followed sixty one undergraduates for thirty days with sleep diaries,\n\n11\n00:00:31,931 --> 00:00:36,277\nand scored how regular each person's sleep was from one day to the next.\n\n12\n00:00:36,799 --> 00:00:40,042\nMore regular sleepers tended to earn higher grades,\n\n13\n00:00:40,306 --> 00:00:43,091\nwith a correlation of about point three seven.\n\n14\n00:00:43,261 --> 00:00:45,310\nThe surprise was the body clock.\n\n15\n00:00:45,696 --> 00:00:47,505\nIn the most irregular sleepers,\n\n16\n00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:51,518\nthe sleep hormone melatonin rose about two and a half hours later.\n\n17\n00:00:51,842 --> 00:00:53,691\nSo a nine a.m.\n\n18\n00:00:53,974 --> 00:00:56,286\nclass could feel more like six a.m.\n\n19\n00:00:56,532 --> 00:01:00,614\nAnd their average sleep length was not significantly different.\n\n20\n00:01:00,784 --> 00:01:02,494\nThe likely driver was light.\n\n21\n00:01:03,039 --> 00:01:07,052\nRegular sleepers got brighter light during the day and less light at night,\n\n22\n00:01:07,346 --> 00:01:09,311\nwhich keeps the body clock anchored.\n\n23\n00:01:09,481 --> 00:01:14,833\nAt MIT, eighty eight chemistry students wore activity trackers for a semester.\n\n24\n00:01:15,407 --> 00:01:20,536\nBetter quality, longer, and more consistent sleep all went with better grades.\n\n25\n00:01:20,871 --> 00:01:26,187\nTogether, sleep measures accounted for nearly a quarter of the variation in scores.\n\n26\n00:01:26,357 --> 00:01:27,846\nAnd the night before a test?\n\n27\n00:01:28,213 --> 00:01:30,688\nIt showed no relation to that test's score.\n\n28\n00:01:31,073 --> 00:01:35,115\nSleep across the week and the month before is what tracked with grades.\n\n29\n00:01:35,452 --> 00:01:38,079\nGoing to bed after about two a.m.\n\n30\n00:01:38,368 --> 00:01:40,172\nalso went with lower scores,\n\n31\n00:01:40,192 --> 00:01:41,987\neven with the same hours.\n\n32\n00:01:42,157 --> 00:01:43,134\nSome caution.\n\n33\n00:01:43,528 --> 00:01:45,498\nBoth studies are correlations,\n\n34\n00:01:45,515 --> 00:01:47,352\neach at a single university.\n\n35\n00:01:47,776 --> 00:01:51,228\nStudents who sleep regularly may differ in other ways too.\n\n36\n00:01:51,543 --> 00:01:53,356\nBut regular timing is cheap,\n\n37\n00:01:53,443 --> 00:01:55,310\nsafe, and in your control.\n\n38\n00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:56,340\nTry this.\n\n39\n00:01:56,683 --> 00:01:59,502\nPick a wake time you can keep seven days a week,\n\n40\n00:01:59,618 --> 00:02:00,835\nweekends included.\n\n41\n00:02:01,206 --> 00:02:03,126\nGet daylight soon after you wake.\n\n42\n00:02:03,603 --> 00:02:07,028\nDim screens and lights in the last hour before bed.\n\n43\n00:02:07,327 --> 00:02:10,663\nAnd protect your sleep across the whole week before an exam,\n\n44\n00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:12,329\nnot just the night before.\n\n45\n00:02:12,499 --> 00:02:14,357\nYour body clock likes routine.\n\n46\n00:02:14,521 --> 00:02:15,405\nGive it one.\n\n"
